Alerting Basics – Detect Problems Before Users Do

🔹 What is Alerting?
Alerting is the process of automatically notifying engineers when a system reaches a defined condition or becomes unhealthy.
👉 Monitoring tells you what is happening.
 👉 Alerting tells you when action is required.

❓ Why Do We Need Alerts?
Production systems run 24/7, so engineers cannot watch dashboards continuously.
Alerts help with:
✔ Early problem detection 🚨
 ✔ Faster incident response ⚡
 ✔ Reduced downtime
 ✔ Better reliability
 ✔ Proactive monitoring

🔔 Common Types of Alerts
🖥️ Infrastructure Alerts
CPU > 90%
Memory usage too high
Disk almost full

🚀 Application Alerts
High error rate
API latency increased
Application unavailable

☸️ Kubernetes Alerts
Pod CrashLoopBackOff
Pod not ready
Node unavailable

💼 Business Alerts
Payment failures increased
Orders dropped
Active users decreased

💡 Real-world Example
An application normally has a 2% error rate.
Suddenly it reaches 15% 🚨
Alert rule triggers:
👉 Error rate > 10% for 5 minutes
📩 DevOps team gets notified
 🔍 Engineers investigate
 🛠️ Issue is fixed
Result → Faster recovery and reduced customer impact. 🚀

🧠 Key Takeaways
✔ Monitoring → Observes the system 📊
 ✔ Alerting → Notifies about problems 🚨
 ✔ Alerts should be actionable 🎯
 ✔ Avoid unnecessary alerts to prevent alert fatigue

💡 Pro Tip:
 A good alert should tell you something is wrong and someone needs to do something about it.
